The error is simple:

Plain Text
fatal: pathspec '.gitignore' did not match any files

This means:

Plain Text
.gitignore file does NOT exist yet

You tried:

Shell
git add .gitignore

before creating the file.

Create .gitignore

Run:

Shell
nano .gitignore

Paste:

.ignore
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
 
.minikube/
.kube/
 
minikube-linux-amd64
 
.env
 
*.log
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
 
.vscode/
.idea/

Save:

Plain Text
CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X
 
Verify File Exists
Shell
ls -la .gitignore

Expected:

Plain Text
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root ...
Add and Commit
Shell
git add .gitignore
 
git commit -m "Add gitignore"
Check if Large Files Are Still Tracked

Run:

Shell
git ls-files | grep venv

and

Shell
git ls-files | grep minikube

If you still see output, then Git history still contains those files.

Best Fix (Recommended)

Since this is a learning project and GitHub already rejected the push because of the large files:

Shell
rm -rf .git

Then:

Shell
git init
 
git branch -M main
 
git add .
 
git commit -m "Initial Commit"
 
git remote add origin https://github.com/hyarelgits/Future-Agentic-Ai.git

Before committing, verify:

Shell
find . -type f -size +100M

Only .gitignore should remain tracked. venv/ and minikube-linux-amd64 should be ignored.

Then push:

Shell
git push -u origin main
 
First run these commands:
Shell
nano .gitignore
 
ls -la .gitignore
 
git status

and confirm .gitignore has been created.
